                "You get the chisel into the seam at the top course, where the slump has left the most room, and you work it, and you keep working it long past the point where it is obviously a waste of an evening.\n\nThen the block shifts. You get your fingers round the edge and walk it out and lower it into the water, and stand there in the cold with your arms shaking, looking at the hole where it was — which is one stone wide, and shows you nothing but more dark.\n\nThere are three more. You are not going to get three more. You know that standing here, and you set the chisel down on the step above the waterline where the next person will see it, and you write nothing, because there is nothing to write except that you were here and you took one out.\n\nThat turns out to be enough to write."
